00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.870
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2019,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.110
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to all of them.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.920
So if you didn't see APIs,
microservices, and service mesh

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.395
talk, then stay tuned,
because here's the rewind.

00:00:13.395 --> 00:00:18.375
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.875 --> 00:00:21.080
What is the relationship
and interplay

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.180
between service
mesh, infrastructure,

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.920
and API management?

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.540
When to use each?

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.100
Let's find out.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.490
Companies are on this journey
of moving from monoliths

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.320
to microservices.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.660
In a microservice's
infrastructure,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.940
an application is
really a collection

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.640
of loosely coupled services.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.880
And we do this to gain
increased product velocity,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.360
manage complexity of
code, admit heterogeneity,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.110
and empower developers.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.970
An alternative to
decomposing systems

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.500
is to wrap them with an API
to make them accessible.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.550
Now, what is an API?

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.870
Well APIs are just
communication contracts.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:07.030
Every system that can
receive requests has an API.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.880
What is a service mesh?

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.590
It's a way to build more
intelligence into the network.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:13.880
Well what does that mean?

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.060
In a monolith, integration
between modules

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.280
was presumed secure.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.250
In a microservices
architecture, the integration

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.520
between services and
request traverse a network.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.170
Therefore, we need mutual
TLS to secure those requests.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.920
But mutual TLS
needs to be managed,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.250
and we need each service
to be able to discover

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.820
the other services that
it might be calling.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.950
And we want services
to all [? adhere ?]

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.760
to communication policies
related to retries, timeouts,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.880
authentication, and we
want all the telemetry data

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.170
from each service.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:56.630
So we need some sort of
system to support all of that.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.690
Let us delegate all of that
complexity of enforcing

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:03.650
the communication protocols
to a standalone proxy known

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.310
as a sidecar proxy,
or the envoy proxy.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.210
This is the model
that Istio uses

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.660
Istio still starts with the
envoy proxy as a sidecar,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.560
and adds a set of pieces to
help manage and provision

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.350
the distributed set of proxies.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.160
Envoy proxy allows you to
manage the inbound calls

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.100
through ingress gateway,
and outbound calls

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.410
through egress gateway.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.530
So you can put the checks
and regulations on inbound

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.750
and the outbound traffic.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.250
This is what we can call a
managed mesh of services.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.750
Well, why use a service mesh?

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.780
It's to connect, secure,
control, and observe

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.760
our microservices
infrastructure.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.570
Now let's talk about
the management of APIs.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.470
As we said, APIs represent
the communication contract.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.280
And as we share APIs to
clients outside the network,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.720
we want to manage requests that
conform to those contracts.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.990
You would use API management
to help developers discover

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.310
the APIs, to modernize your
old legacy APIs into a more

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.260
modern REST-based format, and
to properly report and monitor

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.780
the usage of your APIs.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.390
Let's combine the two
concepts together, now.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.590
Managing microservices
and API management.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.400
We've got our systems, a
legacy system and a monolith.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.370
The internal clients
connect directly.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:35.780
The external clients connect
through the API gateway.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.510
Because we are on the
microservices journey,

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.670
we are decomposing
the monolith into

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.130
multiple distinct services.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.770
And we update the
internal app to keep pace

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.930
with the changing endpoints.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.120
And then, we
introduce new services

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:55.370
to add more capabilities, which
is the onset of complexity.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.400
To simplify this, here's
a clean way of doing it.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.900
We want to use service mesh
infrastructure, like Istio,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.550
to manage the communication
amongst and between services.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.910
And the API management to
manage the communication

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.710
from the clients.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.470
To summarize, here's
the mental model.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.130
You select your infrastructure,
your thousands of services

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:20.990
are built on that layer.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.510
Those services
are to be managed.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:26.930
Some services
expose shared APIs.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:30.770
You need to manage all of
those shared APIs as well.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.800
And clients delivering
end-user experiences then

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.480
use those APIs.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.110
So that's the mental model
that we found to be useful.

